<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#high-king-of-ireland">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">High King of Ireland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ard Rí</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The High King of Ireland, Ard Rí in Irish, was the title claimed by kings who asserted supremacy over the island's many kingdoms. Long associated with Tara and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>, it became a real prize from the late 10th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Bóruma</a> of Munster took it in 1002. The last High King, Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair of Connacht, accepted Henry II's overlordship in 1175.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland.jpg" alt="High King of Ireland" width="266" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brian_Boru,_King_of_Munster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The title Ardrí na hÉireann was held by those who had, or are claimed to have had, lordship over all of Ireland, and it was also given in later writing to figures of myth. Medieval and early modern Irish literature portrays an almost unbroken line of High Kings ruling from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-of-tara">Hill of Tara</a> over lesser kings for thousands of years. Modern historians believe this scheme was crafted in the 8th century from the genealogical traditions of powerful dynasties, to justify their status by projecting it far into the past. John T. Koch writes that it is unlikely that any king had enough authority to dominate the whole island before the 9th century.</p><p>The concept of national kingship is first articulated in the 7th century but became a political reality only in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>, and then not consistently. High Kings never ruled Ireland as a unified state: the High King was an overlord who received tribute from independent kingdoms beneath him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Early Irish kingship was sacred in character. In some early sources High Kings gain power through marriage to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> goddess, and a king free from blemish enforces symbolic prerogatives and avoids symbolic taboos. Diarmait mac Cerbaill, king of Tara in the middle of the 6th century, may have been the last king to marry the land.</p><p>Succession was complicated. Before the Norman takeover of 1171 Ireland was divided into many kingdoms, kings were often succeeded by sons but other branches took turns by agreement or force, and genealogies were later edited. The High King was essentially a ceremonial overlord, with real power only in his own realm. For the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a> this was the Kingdom of Meath.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>, descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niall-of-the-nine-hostages">Niall of the Nine Hostages</a>, had conquered petty kingdoms from the fifth century. Churchmen developed a theory of a high kingship and received royal patronage in return. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> record the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mael-sechnaill-mac-maele-ruanaid">Máel Sechnaill mac Máele Ruanaid</a> in 862 as king of all Ireland, a title his successor Áed Finliath apparently never held, and it is unclear what political reality lay behind it.</p><p>In 1002 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Bóruma</a> of Munster took the high kingship from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mael-sechnaill-mac-domnaill">Máel Sechnaill II</a> of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>. Some historians call it a usurpation, while others say that no one had a strict legal right. Brian was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-clontarf">Battle of Clontarf</a> in 1014, Máel Sechnaill II was restored, and he died in 1022. From then until 1171 the high kingship was held alongside kings with opposition. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obrien-dynasty">Uí Briain</a> of Munster, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-lochlainn-dynasty">Meic Lochlainn</a> of the North and the Uí Conchubhair of Connacht later competed for it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “High King of Ireland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A coalition of native Irish led by Niall Glúndub, <b>High King of Ireland</b>, tried to drive the Uí Ímair Vikings under Sitric Cáech from Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-14">14 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Ruaidrí became the last <b>High King of Ireland</b> and agreed the Treaty of Windsor with Henry II in 1175.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-connacht">Kingdom of Connacht</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 10th century the Dál Cais of Thomond took the kingship, and Brian Bóruma, king of Munster from 978, became <b>High King of Ireland</b> in 1002.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Kingdom of Munster</a></li></ul></section>
